## Comprehensive Summary
Metal organic frameworks have been employed as high-performance layered double hydroxide (LDH) composite supercapacitor electrode materials but have shown unsatisfactory redox ability and stability. Herein, a host-guest CuMo-based polyoxometalate-based metal organic framework (POMOF) with copious electrochemically active sites and strong electrochemical redox activities has been effectively coupled with POM-incorporated CoNi-LDH to develop a nanocomposite (NENU-5@CoNi-LDH) by a simple solvothermal method. The designed electrode shows a high specific capacity of $333.61\mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . In addition, the novel hybrid symmetric supercapacitor NENU-5@CoNi-LDH/active carbon (AC) demonstrated a high energy density of $80.8~\mathsf{W h}\cdot\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ at a power density of ${750.7~\mathsf{W}{\cdot}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}}$ . Interestingly, the nanocomposite of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH exhibits an outstanding capacitance retention of $79\%$ after 5000 charge-discharge cycles at $10\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . This work provides a new strategy and will be the backbone for future energy storage research.
## Background and Originality Content
The current energy crisis and environmental pollution have brought a huge negative impact on human life and world economy. Therefore, many researchers have devoted themselves to exploring new renewable energy sources.[1-2] Among the available energy storage devices, supercapacitors (SCs) are considered to be one of the most promising devices due to their high power density, fast charge-discharge and excellent cycling capability. Therefore, they are widely used in bullet trains and backup power suppliers in powered energy storage devices.[3-4] Polyoxometalates (POMs), as multinuclear transition metal oxide clusters, have been extensively studied in the field of energy storage, catalysis,[5-6] solar photovoltaics,[7-8] and energy conversion with enormous advantages: structural diversity, high thermal stability, and inherent multielectron redox properties.[4,9-10] Especially, $\left[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}\right]^{3-}$ $(\mathsf{P M o}_{12})$ can act as an $^{\prime\prime}24$ electron spone” owing to its large electronic transfer capability.[11-12] However, the low electrical conductivity of electrode materials affects their rate capability, and the high solubility of POMs restricts their application in aqueous supercapacitors.[3] Research communities are encouraged to combine the advantages of metal organic framework (MOF) with the attractive properties of polymer organic framework (POF) in order to achieve high performance supercapacitance. To better utilize the advantages of POMs, the design and synthesis of polyoxometalate-based MOFs (POMOFs) is an efficient approach, which can act as multi-electron redox reaction centre owing to the plentiful active sites and excellent dispersion in solvents. More importantly, the proper porosity, favorable electron channeling, and BET specific surface area in POMOF can enhance the performance of POMOF applications.[9,13-16]
According to the current research and analysis, we found that the applications of POMOFs materials are mainly devoted to the POMOF-derived metal derivative s,[17-18] conductive polymers,[ 19] carbon nanotubes[9] and graphene oxide.[20-21] Although, polyoxometalate-based metal-organic framework-derived layered double hydroxide composites are very rarely investigated, some researchers have used polyoxovanadate anions as pillars to construct LDH composites. For example, Navnath et al. used NiCr-LDH layered hydroxide and polyoxovanadate anions as pillars to construct composite materials for energy storage.[22] The exfoliationrestacked pillar approach yielded mesoporous ordered layered structures with high surface area and interconnected network morphology exhibiting excellent electrochemical performance. It is well known that layered double hydroxide materials (LDHs) have attracted much attention due to their high theoretical expectation, reversible redox kinetics and unique chemical composition.[23-24] In light of the preceding explanation, we attempted to investigate their electrochemical properties by combining POMOFs and LDH materials. This may provide a novel route to achieve high performance supercapacitor in the field of energy storage.
In this study, merging the smart properties of POMOF (NENU-5) and CoNi-LDH, we formulated a facile synthetic strategy to design nanosheet type NENU-5@CoNi-LDH composites by solvothermal methods. NENU-5 consists of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ (HKUST-1) and $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ . HKUST-1 is a well-known porous MOF, in which $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ acts as guest molecule to encapsulate in cavity of HKUST-1 to increase the rigidity of MOFs structure and improve its electrochemical stability. Then, NENU-5 crystals were grown on carbon cloth surface. After adding an appropriate amount of cobalt nitrate and nickel nitrate metal salts, NENU-5@CoNi-LDH composites were generated through in-situ etching and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ ion replacement, and their electrochemical properties were evaluated. The hybrid supercapacitor (HSC) was prepared by NENU-5@CoNi-LDH as the positive electrode and activated carbon (AC) as the negative electrode to research its device performance. Based on our knowledge, for the first time we investigated polyacid-based metalorganic framework-derived layered double hydroxide composites.
## Results and Discussion
The two-step synthesis process for NENU-5@CoNi-LDH on CC is depicted in Figure 1a. First NENU-5 is uniformly grown on the surface of the oxidized CC substrate by a simple solvothermal method using $\mathsf{P M o}_{12},$ $,\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2},$ and ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B T C},$ and after that, the CC substrate works as an adhesive to promote charge mobility. The CC substrate also acts as a support for mechanical flexibility. Secondly, NENU-5 on CC is etched and gradually converted into NENU-5@CoNi-LDH nanocomposite materials through the ions co-precipitation process in $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}$ solution. In general, the synthesis of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH is a process of surface corrosion of NENU-5 and the formation of CoNi-LDH nanosheet.
NENU-5 was grown on the surface of CC and further modified with ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}})_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ to achieve NENU-5@CoNiLDH nanosheet. The sheet structure was confirmed via scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) as shown in Figures 1b—1d. Figures S2 and S3 also display the SEM images of NENU-5 demonstrating octagon configuration. During the second phase of solvothermal reaction in ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2},$ ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2},$ ultrapure water, methanol, and CTAB solution, NENU-5 on CC was gradually transferred to nanosheet structural LDH composite materials, which were then separated from the solution by replacing and etching with ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions. The morphology clearly shows an octahedral 3D nanosheet hierarchical structure. The (015) and (012) facet of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH can be observed in the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Figure 1f). Lattice fringes with interplanar distance of 0.23 and $0.26~\mathsf{n m}$ are consistent with the (015) and (012) crystal plane of CoNi-LDH. The uniform distributions of ${\sf P},$ Mo, Co, Ni, O and Cu elements were confirmed by elemental mapping analysis and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) (Figures 1h, g).
The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis was further carried out to confirm the crystal structure of as-prepared NENU-5, and the diffraction peaks of labeled NENU-5 well match with the previous literature (Figure S1).[25-26] From the XRD pattern of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, NENU-5 and CoNi-LDH peaks can be obviously seen, indicating the successful formation of NENU-5@CoNiLDH nanocomposite. Significantly, the XRD peaks of NENU-5 and HKUST-1 are not distinguished, due to peaks emulsion. In addition, the peaks at $10.1^{\circ}$ , $34.9^{\circ}\ .$ , $43.8^{\circ}$ and $60.1^{\circ}$ are respectively indexed to (003), (012), (015) and (110) planes of the CoNi-LDH (JCPDS card no.40-0216).[27-28] In the XRD pattern, it is worth noting that the four small peaks of NENU-5 corresponding to $11.57^{\circ}$ , $13.51^{\circ}.$ , $\mathsf{16.54^{\circ}}$ , and $\mathsf{17.60}^{\circ}$ can be indexed respectively to the (222), (400), (311), and (511) planes of the HKUST-1/NENU-5.[29-30] These results confirmed that the coexistence of NENU-5 and CoNi-LDH and controlled synthesis condition will result in the partially transformed hybrid composite with NENU- $.5@$ /CoNi-LDH interfaces.
X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) of NENU-5, NENU-5@CoNiLDH and HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH were used to evaluate their surface chemical compositions as well as element valence states. In Figures $S4{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{S6}}_{i}$ the XPS action spectra of the sample NENU-5@CoNiLDH display the coexistence of C, O, Cu, Co, Ni, P and Mo. According to Gaussian peak splitting rule, C 1s is dissolved into three main peaks. The peak at $284.8\ \mathsf{e V}$ is due to $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ band, while the peaks at 285.8 and 288.8 eV are related to $c=c$ and $c=0$ banding (Figures S4b—S6b).[31] For the NENU-5 $@$ CoNi-LDH electrode material, the $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectrum has two peaks of $\mathsf{C u}2\mathsf{p}3/2$ at the binding energy of 932.3 eV and $\mathsf{C u}2\mathsf{p}1/2$ at the binding energy of 952.2 eV. XPS spectra clearly show that the $\mathsf{c u}$ exists in ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ (Figure 2b).[32] The XPS peaks of $\mathtt{C u2p3/2}$ and $\mathsf{C u}2\mathsf{p}1/2$ show an obvious negative shift of ${\sim}1$ eV in $\mathsf{C u2p}$ spectrum, which further confirms the successful coupling between polymetallic electrons. The fitted peaks for Co $2{\mathsf{p}}$ spectrum with the binding energy of 780.3/783.1 eV and 795.9 eV are assigned to the Co $2{\mathsf{p}}3/2$ and Co 2p1/2 spin orbitals of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ in NENU-5@CoNi-LDH (Figure
2c).[33] The Ni 2p spectra of $2{\mathsf{p}}3/2$ and $2{\mathsf{p}}1/2$ are situated at $854.1/856.4\:\mathrm{eV}$ and $871.6/874.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ in Figure 2e showing obvious shifts, which indicated electrochemical coupling in LDH species.[34] Moreover, the binding energies of Co and Ni in NENU-5@CoNiLDH are reduced by $_{0.3-1}$ eV compared with HKUST-1 $@$ CoNiLDH, which suggests the charge transfer between Cu, Co and Ni elements.[35-37] The charge transfer between Cu, Co and Ni elements can improve the electrical conductivity of the electrode material, and further promote the fast electrochemical reaction kinetics and stability of the electrode material. Two peaks at 230.5 eV and 233.6 eV may be attributed to Mo6+ 3d 5/2 and ${\mathsf{M o}}^{6+}$ 3d 3/2, respectively (Figure 2f).[38]
The O 1s spectrum has two sharp peaks at 529.9 eV and 531.4 eV, which are respectively related to M-OH $[{\mathsf{M}}={\mathsf{N i}}$ , Co and Cu) and the hydroxyl groups of water molecules absorbed on the surface of the sample.[37] In addition, the O 1s spectrum of NENU- $.5@$ CoNi-LDH has a high peak intensity at the M-OH peak compared with NENU-5, implying more hydroxide (Figures S4c—S6c).
The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area and pore size distribution of three electrode materials were tested and analyzed from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (Figures 2d and S7). The NENU-5 material had a larger specific surface area of $648.32~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ exhibiting a representative I-isotherm larger than HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH with $40\dot{2}.2~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and NENU-5@CoNi-LDH with $210.2~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ , which may be ascribed to the coating of HKUST-1 and NENU-5 with CoNi-LDH material during etching and ions replacement. Meanwhile, NENU-5@CoNi-LDH and HKUST-1 $@$ CoNi-LDH exhibit characteristics of typical type IV isotherms with an hysteresis loop, suggesting the mesoporous structures, which are attributed to the nanosheet morphology and partial retained HKUST-1/NENU-5 crystalline structure.
## Electrochemical performance
The electrochemical performance of the as-prepared NENU5@CoNi-LDH, HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH and NENU-5 electrode was researched in 6 mol/L KOH electrolyte with a three-electrode system (Figures 3, S8—12). As shown in Figures 3d, S7, the CV curves of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH and other comparison electrode materials were tested at potential window of $_{0-0.6\ V}$ with different scan rates in 6 mol/L KOH electrolyte. CV curves of NENU-5 $@$ CoNi-LDH and HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH show a pair of redox peaks, while the CV curve of NENU-5 displays one oxidation peak and two reduction peaks which are attributed to the multi-electron process of
PMo12 (Figure 3a). Similarly, nano sheet electrode of NENU- $.5@$ CoNi-LDH material has a larger curve area than the other comparative sample electrodes indicating that the as-prepared electrode has a great specific capacitance. To evaluate the electrochemical properties of the as-prepared electrodes, galvanostatic chargedischarge (GCD) curves were investigated at the current density of $1\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 3b). The specific capacities (Qs) were calculated from the GCD curves. Since the composite material NENU- $.5@$ CoNi-LDH possesses high specific capacity $333.61\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at 1 ${\mathsf{A}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}$ which is higher than that of comparison sample HKUST- $\boldsymbol{1}@$ CoNi-LDH $(46.45\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ and NENU-5 $(2.14\ m A\mathsf{h}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ (Figure 3b), it demonstrates the excellent electrochemical capacitance of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH. The rate capabilities of the nanosheet NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH and NENU-5 were calculated by increasing the charge/discharge current density from $1\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ to $10\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and $0.2~\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ to $2\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figures 3f and S9). The reduction in specific capacitance was found to be small, and the $\mathtt{O s}$ of $280.8\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}^{-1}}$ at $10\ \mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for NENU- $.5@$ CoNi-LDH with the retention rate of $84\%$ was investigated, which was higher than HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH $(22.61\:\mathrm{mAh{\cdotg}^{-1},\:48\%)}$ and NENU-5 (2.04 r $\mathsf{n A h{\cdot}g}^{-1},\ 51.4\%)$ (Figure 3f). What’s more, NENU-5 can not only promote the electron transfer, but also provide a large specific surface area and a convenient diffusion path channel for uniform active component of CoNi-LDH. In general, POM (PMo12) is encapsulated in the HKUST-1 framework to increase a favourable stability of framework to improve its electrochemical stability. Furthermore, the outstanding capacity retention rate may be attributable to the synergistic effect of NENU-5 with the electronic transmission channel and the CoNi-LDH with high theoretical capacitance, as well as the unique nanosheet array structure. The improved electrochemical performance and reaction kinetics of the three electrodes were verified by the electrochemical spectroscopy (EIS) measurements in the frequency range from 0.01 to 105. As shown in Figure 3c, the Nyquist plots of NENU-5@CoNiLDH, HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH and NENU-5 composite materials all consist of a semicircle in the high frequency region and a straight line in the low frequency region.[39] In the high-frequency region, the intercept at the X-axis represents the series resistance $\left(R_{s}\right)$ , while the diameter of the semicircle is associated with the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ . The lower $R_{s}$ (0.73 Ω) of NENU-5 than those of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH (1.05 Ω) and HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH $\left(0.89\ \Omega\right)$ implies a lower intrinsic resistance.[40] In addition, the NENU-5@CoNi-LDH electrode has a smaller $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $0.04~\Omega$ lower than those of HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH (0.24 Ω) and NENU-5 $(5.0\ \Omega)$ , suggesting a faster charge transfer kinetics to enhance the electronic conductivity of the composites, and further improved the electrochemical stability. Hence, the smaller the charge transfer resistance, the better the performance of the electrode material. Nevertheless, the straight lines of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, are steeper than those of HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH and NENU-5 in the low frequency region, which indicates that the resultant structure can improve the diffusion of ions into the electrode.[40] The cycle stability of the NENU-5@CoNi-LDH electrode was investigated based on charge/discharge cycles measured at $10\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ and the results are displayed in Figure 4e. NENU-5 $@$ CoNi-LDH has a capacity loss of only $26\%$ after 5000 cycles at a current density of $10\ {\mathsf{A}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}.$ indicating outstanding cycle stability. Furthermore, the morphology of the NENU-5@CoNi-LDH remained intact after electrochemical testing, exhibiting structural stabilities (Figure S13). Finally, these results proved that the unique mesoporous structures of NENU-5 $@$ CoNi-LDH can provide more accessible active metal sites and fast ionic diffusion channels, and the interface of nanosheet thin-layer structure can accelerate transmission of electrons to enhance electrochemical performance and stability.
Generally speaking, the electrochemical energy storage mechanism has two chemical behaviors: the interfacial charge transfer Faraday redox process of ion intercalation and the electric double layer characteristic of charge storage.[41] The CV curve of the scan rate is used to quantify the capacitance contribution of the capacitance control process and the diffusion control process by the following equation:
$$
i(v)=k_{1}v+k_{2}v^{1/2}
$$
$$
\frac{i(v)}{v^{1/2}}=k_{1}v^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
where $i$ is current response, $v$ is scan rate; $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are constants calculated from the CV curves at a different scan rate. When NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH and NENU-5 are used as electrode materials for CV testing, the capacitive contributions are $40\%$ , $40.4\%$ and $89.5\%$ , respectively, indicating that the diffusion-controlled contribution is dominant for NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH composite materials and the capacitive contribution plays a significant role for NENU-5 (Figures 4a and S10). The $b$ values of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH and NENU-5 are calculated as 0.66, 0.71, 0.69, 0.75 and 1, 0.97 for anode and cathode peak, which correspond to the diffusion-contribution battery behavior material and capacitive controlled capacitive material (Figures 4b, S11). Comparing the b values of three different electrode materials, it can be concluded that the $b$ value of the NENU-5@CoNi-LDH electrode material is closer to 0.5 which indicates that it has faster ion diffusion and charge transfer rate. As shown in Figures 4c, S12, the linear relationships between the peak current of anode and cathode and the square root of the scan rate illustrate an excellent reversibility.
## Electrochemical performance of two-electrode system
As displayed in Figure 5a, the HSC device was constructed with NENU-5@CoNi-LDH (positive) and AC (negative) materials (denoted as NENU-5@CoNi-LDH//AC) to evaluate practical applicability. In Figure S14, it exhibits a specific capacity of $318\ \mathsf{F}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at $3\mathsf{A}\cdot\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ by GCD curves of negative electrode. The CV curves of positive and negative electrode were obtained at $10\mathrm{\mV}\cdot\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in which NENU-5@CoNi-LDH shows a clear redox peak in the potential range of $0{-}0.6\ \mathsf{V},$ and AC exhibits a rectangular shape indicating layered capacitance characteristics in the range of 0 to $-1\vee$ (Figure S15). In Figure 5b, it is found that the most suitable operating voltage window for the device is $_{0-1.5\vee}$ . The ${\sf C}_{s}$ of HSC was calculated according to the GCD plots in Figure 5c, and the capacitance value can reach $256.3\ \mathsf{F}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $1\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ Finally, it still retains $108{\mathsf{F}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{g}}^{=1}$ at the $5~{\mathsf{A g}}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, the as-prepared HSC (NENU-5@CoNi-LDH//AC) possesses energy density of $80.8~\mathsf{W h}\cdot\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ at power density of $750.7~\mathsf{W}\cdot\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ and maintains energy density of $23.1\mathsf{W h}{\cdot}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ with a power density of $5489.2~{\mathsf{W}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{k g}}^{-1}.$ which is high than others in POMs-based supercapacitor such as C-POMOF@rGO//AC (20.1 Wh· $\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ with 927 ${\mathsf{W}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{k g}}^{-1})$ , ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{C u@C//A C}}$ (2.6 Wh· ${\mathsf{k g}}^{-1}$ with 790.4 ${\mathsf{W}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{k g}}^{-1})$ , $\{\mathsf{A g}_{6}\mathsf{M o}_{7}\mathsf{O}_{24}\}@\mathsf{A g-M O I}$ F (11.1 Wh· ${\sf k g}^{-1}$ with $600.1~{\mathsf{W}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{k g}}^{-1})$ (Figure 5d). Wherein, the as-prepared HSC NENU-5@CoNi-LDH//AC was retained about $79\%$ after 5000 cycles at $10\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 5e). The decrease in capacity retention may be due to the shedding of active materials on the substrate and the dissolution of the POMs during the test.
## Conclusions
In summary, for designing high performance supercapacitor electrode materials with excellent stability, a host-guest CuMobased POMOF was used as the precursor for the solvothermal construction of POM-incorporated CoNi-LDH nanosheet composite (NENU-5@CoNi-LDH). As an electrode, it exhibited a specific capacity of $333.61\ \mathsf{m A h{\cdot g}}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{1}\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , which is significantly higher than that of the reported POM and $\mathsf{L D H}\mathsf{S C}$ electrode materials. Interestingly, the novel design HSC of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH//AC delivered the high energy density of 80.8 Wh $\mathsf{k}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at a power density of $750.7~\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ with an excellent capacitance retention of $79\%$ after 5000 charge-discharge cycles at $10\mathsf{A}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The key function of the interfacial interaction between the POM and CoNi-LDH in the nanocomposite was confirmed by electrochemical investigation. The hybrids' porosity channels in nanocomposite facilitated ionic diffusion during the electrochemical redox process, resulting in increased capacitance and stability. The current work provides an excellent strategy for convincing high performance electrochemical interfaces in composite.
## Experimental
## Material
Copper nitrate trihydrate $\mathsf{\left(C u(N O_{3})}_{2}{\cdot}3H_{2}O\right)$ , cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}).$ , nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ , and phosphomolybdic acid $(\mathsf{P}\mathsf{M}\mathsf{o}_{12})$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Trimesic acid $(H_{3}B T C)$ , ethanol, methanol, $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), and acetic acid glacial $(C H_{3}C O O H)$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Cetyl trimethyl ammonium bromide (CTAB), potassium hydroxide (KOH) and poly(1,1-difluoroethylene) (PVDF) were obtained from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Carbon cloth (WOS1009) was purchased from Taiwan Ce Tech Co., Ltd. Active carbon was purchased from Kuraray Co., Japan.
## Synthesis of NENU-5 on carbon cloth material
A piece of carbon cloth (CC) of size $1\times2c m^{2}$ was taken in a Teflon-lined stainless autoclave and maintained at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $4h$ by soaking in concentrated nitric acid solution. Then, CC was cleaned by being sonicated in acetone, ethanol and distilled water successively for 15 min. NENU-5 on CC was synthesized by a facile one-step in situ solvothermal method containing $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ $_{(0.49\ \mathsf{g})}$ , $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $(0.49~\mathsf{g})$ , ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B T C}\left(0.24\ {\sf g}\right)$ , in the mixture of ethanol $(5\mathsf{m L})$ , deionized water $(5\mathsf{m L})$ , DMF $(2\mathsf{m L})$ , and HAc (1 mL) under stirring.[25] The above prepared solution and CC were added to 25 mL Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $55^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $\texttt{1d}$ . Finally, NENU-5 was collected by washing with water/ethanol solution and dried at room temperature.
## Synthesis of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH on CC material
The synthesis of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH on CC was realized through solvothermal method by dissolving $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $_{(0.4365\ \mathsf{g})}$ , ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ (0.4361 g), CTAB (1 g) in a mixture of methanol $(60~\mathsf{m L})$ and ultra-pure water $(15~\mathsf{m L})$ under stirring. The above mixture and NENU-5 on CC were added to $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $180~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Ultimately, NENU-5@CoNi-LDH on CC was collected by washing with ethanol solution and dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight. The synthesis of HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH on CC was the same as that of NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, except that phosphomolybdic acid was not added. Similarly, the synthesis of CoNi-LDH on CC was the same as that of HKUST-1@CoNi-LDH, except that HKUST-1 was not added. The loading of electrode material on CC is $1.5~\mathsf{m g}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ .
## Electrochemical test
The electrochemical test of the three-electrode system from CHI660e electrochemical workstation was carried out in $6~\mathsf{m o l/L}$ KOH solution. NENU-5@CoNi-LDH, saturated calomel electrode and platinum sheet were used as working electrode, counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. The tests included cyclic voltammetry curve (CV), constant current charge and discharge curve (GCD), impedance curve (EIS) and long cycle test.
The electrochemical test of the two-electrode system from CHI660e electrochemical workstation was carried out in $6~\mathsf{m o l/L}$ KOH solution. NENU-5@CoNi-LDH and AC were used as anode and cathode. Meanwhile, the preparation of the negative electrode activated carbon was carried out by adding activated carbon:PVDF:acetylene black in a mass ratio of $8:1:1$ . Finally, a few drops of $N_{\l}$ -methylpyrrolidone were added dropwise to grind evenly, and it was in the state of ink. Finally, the pretreated carbon cloth was coated and dried. The loading of AC is about 1.5 mg·cm‒2.
